# First Steps

Open Lince and there is no menu bar, no sidebar, no home screen waiting to be
navigated. There is a canvas — a large surface you pan and zoom — and on it,
small tools called **sands**: a table, a kanban, a record editor, a terminal, a
graph of relations, a transfer panel, a rule builder, a reader for this very
document. You choose which ones exist, where they sit, how big they are, and —
the part that matters most — what data each one is looking at. Nothing here is
a fixed screen, because nobody's work is a fixed screen.

The frame around the canvas is turning into sands as well. Some of it already
is: every new workspace comes with an **Edit** panel pinned to the side, which
is a sand like any other and can be moved, resized or thrown away. The rest —
the small toolbar in the corner with the pencil and the notification bell, the
zoom controls beside it — is still drawn by the host. The direction is one
Lince rather than an application with a canvas inside it, so that the parts you
would normally call "the program" end up the same kind of thing as the parts
you build for yourself.

A **workspace** is one canvas with its arrangement of sands. Keep several, name
them, switch between them, throw one away when it has served its purpose —
nothing underneath is lost, because a sand is a window onto your records, never
a box holding them. The same record can appear on four boards at once and it is
still one record.

The ideas below walk through the frontend in order: the toolbar in the corner,
what edit mode turns on, the two layers a sand can live in, how to add one, and
then **Protein** — the thing that decides which records a sand is looking at,
which is where the frontend stops being about windows and starts being about
your data.

Everything after this branch is what sits underneath. First why Lince exists at
all, then the model everything is made from, then the boundary between one
Lince and another, the transfers that cross it, and the rules that keep working
while you are not looking.
